{"module":"labor","voice":"male","duration":222.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Labor: work, unions and the fight for fair pay","start":0.0,"end":3.67,"words":[[0.05,0.55],[0.84,1.17],[1.46,2.04],[2.04,2.29],[2.29,2.46],[2.46,2.79],[2.79,2.95],[2.95,3.2],[3.2,3.45]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Slavery was a labor system, and much of what followed was about controlling Black labor.","start":4.47,"end":10.68,"words":[[4.52,5.12],[5.12,5.35],[5.35,5.5],[5.5,5.87],[5.87,6.4],[6.66,6.89],[6.89,7.19],[7.19,7.34],[7.34,7.57],[7.57,8.02],[8.02,8.25],[8.25,8.62],[8.62,9.38],[9.38,9.68],[9.68,10.13]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The fight for fair work has also been one of the movement's strongest engines.","start":11.03,"end":15.79,"words":[[11.07,11.22],[11.22,11.51],[11.51,11.65],[11.65,11.87],[11.87,12.09],[12.09,12.31],[12.31,12.67],[12.67,12.89],[12.89,13.11],[13.11,13.26],[13.26,13.4],[13.4,13.99],[13.99,14.64],[14.64,15.23]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Sharecropping and debt peonage","start":16.14,"end":18.37,"words":[[16.18,16.99],[16.99,17.26],[17.26,17.53],[17.53,18.17]]},{"el":"s0","text":"After emancipation, most Black families in the South farmed land they didn't own, paying with a share of the crop and buying on credit from the landowner's store.","start":19.07,"end":29.82,"words":[[19.12,19.45],[19.45,20.51],[20.79,21.2],[21.2,21.53],[21.53,22.1],[22.1,22.26],[22.26,22.43],[22.43,22.75],[22.75,23.08],[23.08,23.41],[23.41,23.65],[23.65,24.06],[24.06,24.39],[24.67,25.08],[25.08,25.33],[25.33,25.49],[25.49,25.73],[25.73,25.9],[25.9,26.06],[26.06,26.39],[26.39,26.63],[26.63,27.04],[27.04,27.2],[27.2,27.7],[27.7,28.02],[28.02,28.19],[28.19,28.92],[28.92,29.25]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Many fell into debts they could never repay.","start":30.17,"end":32.63,"words":[[30.21,30.46],[30.46,30.64],[30.64,30.89],[30.89,31.13],[31.13,31.32],[31.32,31.5],[31.5,31.75],[31.75,32.12]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Congress outlawed debt peonage in 1867, and the Supreme Court struck down an Alabama law that criminalized quitting while in debt in Bailey v. Alabama (1911), but forced labor through debt persisted for decades.","start":32.98,"end":47.99,"words":[[33.02,33.57],[33.57,34.04],[34.04,34.27],[34.27,34.82],[34.82,34.97],[34.97,36.3],[36.57,36.81],[36.81,36.96],[36.96,37.43],[37.43,37.66],[37.66,38.05],[38.05,38.37],[38.37,38.52],[38.52,39.07],[39.07,39.22],[39.22,39.46],[39.46,40.4],[40.4,40.86],[40.86,41.18],[41.18,41.33],[41.33,41.57],[41.57,41.72],[41.72,42.11],[42.11,42.35],[42.74,43.28],[43.28,44.38],[44.65,44.88],[44.88,45.19],[45.19,45.58],[45.58,45.82],[45.82,46.05],[46.05,46.68],[46.68,46.83],[46.83,47.46]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Unions: shut out, then organized","start":48.89,"end":52.54,"words":[[48.94,49.78],[50.14,50.45],[50.45,50.87],[51.23,51.54],[51.54,52.38]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Many early craft unions in the American Federation of Labor excluded Black workers, and employers then used excluded Black workers as strikebreakers, deepening division.","start":53.24,"end":64.11,"words":[[53.29,53.58],[53.58,53.8],[53.8,54.16],[54.16,54.67],[54.67,54.81],[54.81,54.96],[54.96,55.54],[55.54,56.27],[56.27,56.41],[56.41,56.78],[56.78,57.43],[57.43,57.72],[57.72,58.16],[58.41,58.63],[58.63,59.21],[59.21,59.43],[59.43,59.72],[59.72,60.37],[60.37,60.66],[60.66,61.03],[61.03,61.17],[61.17,62.19],[62.44,62.95],[62.95,63.53]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In the 1930s the Congress of Industrial Organizations organized whole industries across racial lines.","start":64.46,"end":71.2,"words":[[64.51,64.65],[64.65,64.79],[64.79,65.63],[65.63,65.77],[65.77,66.26],[66.26,66.4],[66.4,67.18],[67.18,68.09],[68.09,68.65],[68.65,68.93],[68.93,69.56],[69.56,69.92],[69.92,70.34],[70.34,70.76]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Arkansas, the Southern Tenant Farmers Union (1934) organized Black and white sharecroppers together, 15 years after the Elaine massacre had punished Black farmers for trying (see “Burned, flooded and taken”).","start":71.55,"end":85.83,"words":[[71.6,71.76],[71.76,72.32],[72.6,72.76],[72.76,73.16],[73.16,73.64],[73.64,74.04],[74.04,74.52],[74.52,75.56],[75.84,76.48],[76.48,76.8],[76.8,77.04],[77.04,77.36],[77.36,78.08],[78.08,78.64],[78.92,79.4],[79.4,79.72],[79.72,80.04],[80.04,80.2],[80.2,80.6],[80.6,81.08],[81.08,81.32],[81.32,81.8],[81.8,82.12],[82.12,82.52],[82.52,82.68],[82.68,83.16],[83.16,83.32],[83.32,83.72],[84.0,84.48],[84.48,84.72],[84.72,85.28]]},{"el":"h2","text":"The Pullman porters","start":86.73,"end":88.37,"words":[[86.77,86.99],[86.99,87.63],[87.63,88.17]]},{"el":"s2","text":"A.","start":89.07,"end":89.65,"words":[[89.12,89.23]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Philip Randolph founded the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters in 1925.","start":90.0,"end":95.1,"words":[[90.04,90.39],[90.39,90.88],[90.88,91.37],[91.37,91.51],[91.51,92.08],[92.08,92.22],[92.22,92.64],[92.64,92.78],[92.78,93.13],[93.13,93.27],[93.27,94.54]]},{"el":"s2","text":"After 12 years, it won a contract with the Pullman Company in 1937, the first major contract between a Black-led union and a large U.S. corporation.","start":95.45,"end":106.63,"words":[[95.5,95.79],[95.79,96.15],[96.15,96.52],[96.78,96.92],[96.92,97.14],[97.14,97.29],[97.29,97.88],[97.88,98.1],[98.1,98.24],[98.24,98.68],[98.68,99.19],[99.19,99.34],[99.34,100.59],[100.84,100.99],[100.99,101.28],[101.28,101.72],[101.72,102.31],[102.31,102.75],[102.75,102.89],[102.89,103.41],[103.41,103.85],[103.85,104.07],[104.07,104.21],[104.21,104.51],[104.51,104.87],[105.24,106.04]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1941 Randolph threatened a march on Washington of 100,000 Black workers; to prevent it, President Roosevelt issued Executive Order 8802, banning discrimination in the defense industries.","start":106.98,"end":118.83,"words":[[107.02,107.13],[107.13,107.94],[107.94,108.35],[108.35,108.75],[108.75,108.87],[108.87,109.1],[109.1,109.22],[109.22,109.68],[109.68,109.79],[109.79,110.78],[110.78,111.01],[111.01,111.36],[111.56,111.68],[111.68,112.08],[112.08,112.25],[112.46,112.98],[112.98,113.5],[113.5,113.73],[113.73,114.31],[114.31,114.48],[114.48,115.93],[116.13,116.42],[116.42,117.23],[117.23,117.35],[117.35,117.46],[117.46,117.81],[117.81,118.39]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Randolph led the 1963 March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom.","start":119.18,"end":124.1,"words":[[119.23,119.74],[119.74,119.95],[119.95,120.1],[120.1,121.26],[121.26,121.54],[121.54,121.69],[121.69,122.27],[122.27,122.41],[122.41,122.77],[122.77,122.99],[122.99,123.5]]},{"el":"h3","text":"“I AM A MAN”: Memphis, 1968","start":125.0,"end":128.31,"words":[[125.04,125.19],[125.19,125.34],[125.34,125.49],[125.49,125.79],[126.06,126.58],[126.85,128.13]]},{"el":"s3","text":"On February 1, 1968, Memphis sanitation workers Echol Cole and Robert Walker were crushed to death by a faulty garbage truck.","start":129.01,"end":138.96,"words":[[129.06,129.23],[129.23,129.89],[129.89,130.22],[130.51,131.99],[132.28,132.78],[132.78,133.6],[133.6,134.02],[134.02,134.35],[134.35,134.68],[134.68,134.93],[134.93,135.34],[135.34,135.67],[135.67,135.84],[135.84,136.25],[136.25,136.41],[136.41,136.66],[136.66,136.91],[136.91,137.07],[137.07,137.49],[137.49,137.98],[137.98,138.4]]},{"el":"s3","text":"About 1,300 Black sanitation workers went on strike for safety, pay and union recognition, carrying signs that read “I AM A MAN.”","start":139.31,"end":148.78,"words":[[139.36,139.72],[139.72,141.16],[141.16,141.45],[141.45,142.17],[142.17,142.53],[142.53,142.82],[142.82,142.96],[142.96,143.39],[143.39,143.54],[143.54,144.04],[144.29,144.51],[144.51,144.73],[144.73,145.16],[145.16,145.95],[146.2,146.71],[146.71,147.07],[147.07,147.28],[147.28,147.5],[147.5,147.64],[147.64,147.79],[147.79,147.93],[147.93,148.22]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Dr. King came to support them and was assassinated there on April 4.","start":149.13,"end":154.0,"words":[[149.18,149.61],[149.97,150.19],[150.19,150.48],[150.48,150.62],[150.62,150.98],[150.98,151.19],[151.19,151.41],[151.41,151.62],[151.62,152.41],[152.41,152.63],[152.63,152.77],[152.77,153.2],[153.2,153.42]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The city settled with the union on April 16.","start":154.35,"end":157.82,"words":[[154.4,154.55],[154.55,154.84],[154.84,155.28],[155.28,155.5],[155.5,155.65],[155.65,156.09],[156.09,156.24],[156.24,156.68],[156.68,157.27]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Left out of the law","start":158.72,"end":160.67,"words":[[158.77,159.3],[159.3,159.69],[159.69,159.96],[159.96,160.22],[160.22,160.49]]},{"el":"s4","text":"New Deal labor protections excluded farm and domestic workers, jobs most Black Southern workers held (see “Wealth”).","start":161.37,"end":168.89,"words":[[161.41,161.65],[161.65,161.88],[161.88,162.27],[162.27,163.06],[163.06,163.76],[163.76,164.0],[164.0,164.23],[164.23,164.86],[164.86,165.33],[165.61,166.0],[166.0,166.39],[166.39,166.71],[166.71,167.1],[167.1,167.49],[167.49,167.8],[167.8,167.96],[167.96,168.35]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Domestic workers organized anyway: Dorothy Bolden founded the National Domestic Workers Union in Atlanta in 1968, and New York passed the first Domestic Workers' Bill of Rights in 2010.","start":169.24,"end":181.71,"words":[[169.29,169.87],[169.87,170.22],[170.22,170.79],[170.79,171.29],[171.54,171.97],[171.97,172.47],[172.47,172.97],[172.97,173.12],[173.12,173.62],[173.62,174.19],[174.19,174.54],[174.54,174.97],[174.97,175.11],[175.11,175.61],[175.61,175.76],[175.76,177.04],[177.29,177.51],[177.51,177.72],[177.72,177.94],[177.94,178.22],[178.22,178.36],[178.36,178.65],[178.65,179.22],[179.22,179.58],[179.58,179.79],[179.79,179.94],[179.94,180.29],[180.29,180.44],[180.44,181.15]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Work today","start":182.61,"end":183.61,"words":[[182.65,182.94],[182.94,183.41]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Black workers have the highest union membership rate of any racial group (Bureau of Labor Statistics), and the wage boost from union membership is larger for Black workers than for white workers (Economic Policy Institute).","start":184.31,"end":199.25,"words":[[184.36,184.7],[184.7,185.12],[185.12,185.37],[185.37,185.54],[185.54,186.05],[186.05,186.56],[186.56,187.23],[187.23,187.57],[187.57,187.74],[187.74,187.99],[187.99,188.5],[188.5,188.83],[188.83,189.43],[189.43,189.59],[189.59,190.02],[190.02,190.94],[191.24,191.49],[191.49,191.66],[191.66,192.08],[192.08,192.42],[192.42,192.76],[192.76,193.27],[193.27,193.94],[193.94,194.11],[194.11,194.53],[194.53,194.7],[194.7,195.04],[195.04,195.46],[195.46,195.71],[195.71,195.88],[195.88,196.22],[196.22,196.64],[196.64,197.32],[197.32,197.82],[197.82,198.67]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Farmworkers in Immokalee, Florida, most of them Latino, Haitian and Guatemalan, won the Fair Food Program in 2011, which buyers such as major fast-food chains and grocers have joined to guarantee better pay and protections.","start":199.6,"end":217.75,"words":[[199.61,200.32],[200.32,200.5],[200.5,201.21],[201.52,202.23],[202.54,202.98],[202.98,203.16],[203.16,203.42],[203.42,204.13],[204.44,204.97],[204.97,205.24],[205.24,206.21],[206.52,206.79],[206.79,206.97],[206.97,207.23],[207.23,207.5],[207.5,208.21],[208.21,208.39],[208.39,209.63],[209.94,210.29],[210.29,210.73],[210.73,211.09],[211.09,211.27],[211.27,211.8],[211.8,212.42],[212.42,212.95],[212.95,213.21],[213.21,213.83],[213.83,214.1],[214.1,214.63],[214.63,214.81],[214.81,215.43],[215.43,215.78],[215.78,216.05],[216.05,216.32],[216.32,217.29]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Fair hiring is covered in “Equal or better.”","start":218.1,"end":221.33,"words":[[218.15,218.41],[218.41,219.01],[219.01,219.18],[219.18,219.61],[219.61,219.78],[219.78,220.21],[220.21,220.3],[220.3,220.72]]}]}